<!--
# Hello Lambda

Deploys `examples/beetmania` as an http-only AWS Lambda (behind an API Gateway URL,
no custom domain). The generic `beet` binary is packaged as a `provided.al2023`
function and reads the site from an S3 bucket at request time (the zip's
`bootstrap` script launches it with `--package=s3://<bucket> --server=http serve`,
since the lambda runtime offers no argv, `serve` being the example's command
that IS the site), so a site change re-publishes by re-running `sync` with no
redeploy. Run `just beetmania assets` first, so the icons, link previews and
images the site borrows and derives are in the tree it publishes.

## Usage

    beet --entry=examples/infra/lambda.bsx validate
    beet --entry=examples/infra/lambda.bsx deploy
    beet --entry=examples/infra/lambda.bsx sync
    beet --entry=examples/infra/lambda.bsx watch
    beet --entry=examples/infra/lambda.bsx destroy --force

A one-shot `CliServer` root whose `CallOnReady` fires the CLI request on load, with
the `Router` as its dispatch child. A `CliServer` refuses a flag the verb does
not take, so `destroy --froce` asks `did you mean --force?` rather than running.
`<Stack app_name="lambda">` is the resource identity every block and verb beneath
it resolves by ancestry, and
`<DeployRoutes deploy=.. destroy=../>` adds the standard IaC verb routes
(deploy/destroy/validate/plan/apply/...) to it.

`deploy` and `destroy` run the two `<Group>`s below, forward and in reverse: a
group is an ordered list of members, and a teardown is that same list read
backwards, so the bucket that is created first is removed last. The other verbs
are child-sequenced `<Route path=".." {ExchangeSequence}>`: their actions are
direct children of the route and the sequence runs them in order.

Live deploy needs AWS credentials in the environment, plus `tofu`,
`cargo-zigbuild` and `zip`.
-->
<CliServer {CallOnReady}>
<Router {(RequestLogger, HelpHandler)}>
	<RequireCfg cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra"/>
	<Stack app_name="lambda" {AwsRegion("us-west-2")}>
		<Group bx:ref="deploy_up">
			<!-- the block + its build artifact share one entity (the deploy pairs them) -->
			<LambdaSiteBlock features="lambda" exec_route="serve"/>
			<!-- deploy-versioned by default: each deploy publishes the site under
			     its own id and ships a binary baked to read that prefix -->
			<S3BucketBlock label="package" {PackageStoreBlock}/>
			<!-- the bucket first, so the site is in place before the function that
			     serves it exists -->
			<TofuApply layer="storage"/>
			<!-- publish the site: assemble it, then one mirror into this deploy's
			     version of the package store -->
			<!-- the wasm runtime the site's pages boot, built by this verb rather
			     than borrowed: the assemble reads it through the site's `wasm/` links -->
			<WasmRuntime cargo_package="beet-cli" bin="beet" features="web_ui" out="target/wasm/beet-ui.wasm"/>
			<PackageAssemble src="examples/beetmania"/>
			<PackageSync/>
			<TofuApply/>
			<!-- the rollout is quick, so tail briefly then exit -->
			<LambdaWatch timeout="30s"/>
		</Group>
		<Group bx:ref="deploy_down">
			<StackTeardown/>
			<TofuDestroy/>
		</Group>
		<DeployRoutes deploy={$deploy_up} destroy={$deploy_down}/>
		<!-- a content-only republish, into the version the running service was
		     built against rather than a fresh one nothing reads -->
		<Route path="sync" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<AdoptCurrentDeploy/>
			<!-- the wasm runtime the site's pages boot, built by this verb rather
			     than borrowed: the assemble reads it through the site's `wasm/` links -->
			<WasmRuntime cargo_package="beet-cli" bin="beet" features="web_ui" out="target/wasm/beet-ui.wasm"/>
			<PackageAssemble src="examples/beetmania"/>
			<PackageSync/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="watch" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<LambdaWatch/>
		</Route>
	</Stack>
</Router>
</CliServer>
